### Static nested class

A static nested class is declared with the `static` keyword inside another class. It does not hold any reference to an outer class instance, so it behaves like a top-level class that is namespaced inside the outer class. The most common use is the Builder pattern.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class Account {
    private final String id;
    private final BigDecimal balance;

    private Account(String id, BigDecimal balance) {
        this.id = id;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    // Static nested: no reference to any Account instance
    public static class Builder {
        private String id;
        private BigDecimal balance = BigDecimal.ZERO;

        public Builder id(String id) { this.id = id; return this; }
        public Builder balance(BigDecimal b) { this.balance = b; return this; }
        public Account build() { return new Account(id, balance); }
    }
}

// Create without any Account instance
Account a = new Account.Builder()
        .id("SAV2001")
        .balance(new BigDecimal("500000"))
        .build();
```

The Builder class is `static`, so you create it with `new Account.Builder()` – no Account object is needed.

### Inner class (non-static)

An inner class (non-static nested class) holds an implicit reference to the enclosing outer instance. It can access all of the outer instance's fields, including private ones.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Account {
    private List<String> transactions = new ArrayList<>();

    // Inner class: has access to Account's private fields
    public class TransactionIterator {
        private int index = 0;
        public boolean hasNext() { return index < transactions.size(); }
        public String next() { return transactions.get(index++); }
    }

    public TransactionIterator iterator() {
        return new TransactionIterator();  // implicitly tied to this Account
    }
}

// Usage: needs an Account instance
Account a = new Account();
Account.TransactionIterator it = a.iterator();

// Or using the outer.new syntax:
Account.TransactionIterator it2 = a.new TransactionIterator();
```

The inner class has an implicit `Account.this` reference. You can access it explicitly: `Account.this.transactions`.

### Local class (inside a method)

A local class is defined inside a method body. It can access the method's local variables, but only if they are effectively final (never reassigned after initialization).

```java
package com.bank.report;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class ReportGenerator {
    public void generateSummary(List<BigDecimal> balances) {
        final String bankName = "National Bank of India";

        // Local class defined inside a method
        class SummaryFormatter {
            String format(BigDecimal total) {
                return bankName + " – Total: " + total;  // captures bankName
            }
        }

        BigDecimal total = balances.stream().reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);
        SummaryFormatter formatter = new SummaryFormatter();
        System.out.println(formatter.format(total));
    }
}
```

Local classes are rare in modern Java because lambdas handle most of these use cases more concisely.

### Anonymous class vs lambda

An anonymous class is an unnamed class defined inline. Before Java 8, this was the standard way to implement callbacks and event handlers. Since Java 8, lambdas replace most anonymous class use cases for functional interfaces.

```java
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;

// Anonymous class (pre-Java 8)
Collections.sort(accounts, new Comparator<Account>() {
    @Override
    public int compare(Account a, Account b) {
        return a.getBalance().compareTo(b.getBalance());
    }
});

// Lambda (Java 8+): replaces anonymous class for functional interfaces
accounts.sort((a, b) -> a.getBalance().compareTo(b.getBalance()));

// Or method reference – the cleanest form
accounts.sort(Comparator.comparing(Account::getBalance));
```

Anonymous classes are still needed when you need to implement an interface with multiple methods or extend a class.

### When to use which?

| Type | Use when |
|---|---|
| Static nested | Logically grouped with outer, no outer reference needed (Builder, Entry, Node) |
| Inner class | Needs access to outer instance's state (Iterator, Handler) |
| Local class | Need a named class inside one method (rare – lambdas are usually better) |
| Anonymous class | One-off implementation of interface/abstract class (replaced by lambdas for SAM) |
| Lambda | Functional interface implementation (prefer this since Java 8) |

### What are common mistakes?

The most dangerous mistake is creating a non-static inner class in a context where it accidentally prevents the outer object from being garbage collected.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class NotificationService {
    private byte[] largeBuffer = new byte[10_000_000];  // 10 MB

    // WRONG: non-static inner class holds a reference to NotificationService
    public class Handler implements Runnable {
        @Override
        public void run() {
            System.out.println("Handling notification");
        }
    }

    // The Handler holds a hidden reference to the NotificationService.
    // If Handler is stored in a long-lived collection, the NotificationService
    // and its 10 MB buffer cannot be garbage collected.
}

// FIX: make it static
public static class Handler implements Runnable {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Handling notification");
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Inner classes hold a hidden reference** to the outer instance. This can prevent GC of the outer object and cause memory leaks. Android development famously had memory leaks from non-static inner classes in Activities.
- **Static nested is preferred** unless you genuinely need the outer reference.
- **Map.Entry** is a static nested interface inside Map. `HashMap.Node` is a static nested class.
- **Records can be nested:** `record AccountSummary(String id, BigDecimal balance) {}` inside a class. Nested records are implicitly static.
- **Lambdas capture the enclosing `this`** if they reference instance members, similar to inner classes. This can also cause retention issues.
- **Serialization of inner classes** is discouraged because the synthetic fields (like the outer reference) create hidden coupling.
